# bash-sigpipe  (generated by harness/probes.py)
Q: a hook writes to a sink that died. Does the shell survive?

Setup: FD 3 is a process-substitution pipe whose reader (`true`) exits
immediately, so every later write hits a closed pipe.

raw write to dead pipe             shell_survived=False SIGPIPE_died=True  output='echo STILL-ALIVE'
write inside trap '' PIPE          shell_survived=True  SIGPIPE_died=False output='echo STILL-ALIVE\nSTILL-ALIVE\n⟦LI-P⟧'
write from a subshell              shell_survived=True  SIGPIPE_died=False output='echo STILL-ALIVE\nSTILL-ALIVE\n⟦LI-P⟧'
python connect to dead socket      shell_survived=True  SIGPIPE_died=False output='echo STILL-ALIVE\nSTILL-ALIVE\n⟦LI-P⟧'

CONCLUSION: a raw write to a dead pipe KILLS the interactive shell -- the
hook must never trust the sink. `trap '' PIPE` around the write, writing
from a subshell, or a connect() that fails cleanly all survive. A daemon
that has gone away must never be able to take the user's shell with it.
